    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although numerous studies are available for well test analysis in closed depletion type reservoirs, the constant pressure condition at the drainage boundary of a well (under either active water-drive or fluid injection), has received only limited attention in the petroleum literature. Consequently, in the present investigation both a single-well and a multi-well system with constant pressure at the outer boundary are studied.To introduce the subject, well test behavior of a well in the center of a constant pressure square is considered, first, in detail. Two methods are presented to estimate the initial pressure and the drainage area average pressure: (1) by reading shut-in pressure corresponding to an appropriate shut-in time, and (2) from plots of the Matthews-Brons-Hazebroek Functions. It is shown that the Miller-Dyes-Hutchinson curve in this case normally provides the initial pressure and not the drainage area average pressure.A system of two wells in a 2:1 rectangle with one short side at constant pressure is studied next. The drainage boundary between the two wells is determined as a function of flow rate ratio for the two wells, and as function of dimensionless producing times. The drainage area average pressure is computed for each well as a function of producing time, and related to initial reservoir pressure and producing well pressure. At steady state, each well contacts a portion of the constant pressure boundary approximately proportional to the production rate expressed as fraction of the total (two wells) producing rate.This study employs the superposition principle to generate the pressure drawdown and buildup behavior at a well in both the above mentioned systems. Synthetic buildup graphs of the Horner, Miller-Dyes-Hutchinson, and Muskat types for various producing times are computed, and used to establish rules for proper interpretation of water-drive buildups. It is found that with the early shut-in data, both the Horner and Miller-Dyes-Hutchinson graphs are equally useful at long producing times, while the Horner graph is recommended at short producing times. The Muskat graph is preferable with late shut-in data for any length well producing time.",
